WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.390
The robust robotics group
under the leadership

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.570
of professors Nick Roy and
Seth Teller at the MIT Computer

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.880
Science and Artificial
Intelligence Laboratory

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.040
is developing a voice
commandable autonomous

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.180
wheelchair.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.040
This video shows a
research prototype

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.260
under development since 2005.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.880
The long term goals
of the project

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.340
are to develop intelligent
assistant technology that

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.200
can work with a
variety of people,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.610
and the wheelchair
is one example

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.500
of this kind of technology.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.300
Our target population
consists of people

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.310
who have limited physical
mobility and limited

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.780
physical control.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.270
By adding a voice commandable
interface and intelligent

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.160
autonomy to a
person's wheelchair,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.690
the chair can then
be used by people

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.540
who may not have the necessary
level of physical ability

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.910
to control the wheelchair
using a standard interface,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.860
such as a joystick.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.290
We are developing a
range of capabilities

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.360
from simply augmenting
existing wheelchairs

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.420
to developing a fully
autonomous powered wheelchair.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.120
We are collaborating with the
Boston Home, a specialized care

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.280
residence for adults with
progressive neurological

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.690
diseases.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.570
And working with TBH, we
plan to develop wheelchairs

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.489
that can be used by
more TBH residents

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.590
without assistance
from a caregiver,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.630
increasing the independence
and quality of life

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.837
of the residents.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.080
Our prototype is a commodity
powered wheelchair.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.380
But we have added a number of
components, such as a low power

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.440
laser range scanner to
detect obstacles and provide

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.620
fine grained position tracking
for autonomous control.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.300
We have added a
microphone to listen

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.310
to the wheelchair
user and speakers

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.137
to respond using speech.

00:01:32.137 --> 00:01:33.970
On this wheelchair, we
have added a computer

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.000
for sensory processing and
intelligent decisioning making.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.470
When the wheelchair
does not need

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.440
to provide fully autonomous
navigation and control,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.980
we can deploy a small PDA
tablet instead of a full sensor

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.480
suite and computer.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.630
The smaller tablet can easily be
added to existing wheelchairs,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.820
but still allows us to
provide a voice interaction

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.580
so that the wheelchair can
give information to its user,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.040
for example, about
daily activities.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.992
The tablet also provides
Wi-Fi connectivity

00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:00.700
that allows it to
estimate the wheelchair

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.190
position at a room
level and the estimated

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.600
position to caregivers.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.270
So the nurses can more easily
stay aware of the wheelchair

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.430
users health and needs.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.820
The onboard computer or tablet
can process speech and respond

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.010
via speech to user queries.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.800
In the future, we plan to use
the tablet as an alternate way

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.810
to interact with
wheelchair users

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.360
for populations who have
difficulty with speech.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.430
For full autonomous
navigation and control,

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.500
the wheelchair can use
a laser range scanner

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.740
to detect obstacles and estimate
its position in the map.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.930
This Hokuyo laser
range scanner sends out

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.460
a plane of infrared
light, which it

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.340
uses to measure the distance
to obstacles around it.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.130
By comparing the positions of
obstacles around the wheelchair

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.380
to the positions of
obstacles in a map,

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.750
the wheelchair can estimate
its position in a map

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.250
as it moves around.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.230
The wheelchair can use
the same laser sensor

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.020
to explore new environments
and build new maps.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.060
Even if an object
is not in the map,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.880
the sensor allows the wheelchair
to detect the new obstacle

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.290
and replant around it.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.810
The technologies for autonomous
navigation and control

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.600
are not new, although, they
have not been widely deployed

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.280
on autonomous wheelchairs.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.432
Our project has a number
of innovative aspects

00:03:05.432 --> 00:03:07.390
that are designed to make
autonomous navigation

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.670
and control more easily
deployed and adopted

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.050
by the general public.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.930
For example, we are building
on well-known robot mapping

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.660
algorithms that can learn the
floor plan of environments

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.160
and developing
algorithms that allow

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.743
a wheelchair to learn
new environments

00:03:19.743 --> 00:03:22.210
and build maps from a
narrative guided tour.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.710
The wheelchair can
take instructions

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:27.010
from a human caregiver to learn
the names of places in the map.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.480
This natural language
understanding

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.105
will make it much
easier for caregivers

00:03:30.105 --> 00:03:32.320
to deploy the wheelchair
in new environments

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.849
and make it easier for
users to use the wheelchair.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.190
Once the chair understands the
user's desired destination,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:58.130
it navigates a route through
the previously learned map

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.062
to that intended destination.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.275
Go down the hallway past the
door and along the railing,

00:04:08.275 --> 00:04:09.650
until you get to
the refrigerator

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.845
in the kitchen on your right.

00:04:11.845 --> 00:04:13.220
We are also
developing algorithms

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.870
that will allow the
robot to be guided

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.700
through unfamiliar environments.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.950
For example, the robot
can follow a simple spoken

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.350
directions to an unlabeled
location in the map

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.840
by reasoning about how
people use everyday objects

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.330
as landmarks for navigation.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.320
For a user with
difficulty communicating,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.050
the chair can learn the user's
behavior and mobility patterns

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:33.050
over time in order to
improve ease of use.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:35.570
For example, when the
user has a daily routine,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.780
the chair can anticipate the
user's desired destinations

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.700
and plan routes
there proactively,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.270
reducing the user burden fully
specifying each destination.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:47.310
As we add more wheelchairs with
knowledge of where they are,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.550
we can give residents
of the Boston Home

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.280
the ability to find each other.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.950
The Intelligent
Wheelchair Project.

